{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":243676,"duration_api_ms":242217,"num_turns":28,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"1ee71f8c-6366-4459-9004-1907ab6cdb93","total_cost_usd":1.03228825,"usage":{"input_tokens":22,"cache_creation_input_tokens":53783,"cache_read_input_tokens":1086469,"output_tokens":6112,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":53783,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":22,"outputTokens":6112,"cacheReadInputTokens":1086469,"cacheCreationInputTokens":53783,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.03228825,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"amendments":[{"body":"The `hatch_produces_juveniles` test in `crates/tank_core/tests/shrimp_population.rs:110-137` is failing. After the branch's chemistry/nitrogen cycle changes, the test setup no longer maintains good enough water conditions for shrimp reproduction — TAN reaches 2.168 mg/L (toxic) and population drops from 10 to 2 adults with 0 juveniles. Either the test's water chemistry setup needs retuning (higher nitrifier biomass, more frequent water changes, or lower feeding) to match the updated simulation dynamics, or there is a regression in the nitrogen cycle that causes ammonia to accumulate excessively despite the already-boosted nitrifier params.","rationale":"This is a correctness failure — a test that the branch itself modifies now fails, meaning either the test expectations or the simulation logic need adjustment before merge. The test is specifically designed to validate that shrimp can reproduce under 'good conditions', but the conditions are no longer good under the new chemistry model."},{"body":"Migration functions use an infallible signature `fn(&mut Value)` (save.rs:29). Future migrations planned for phases B7 (nitrogen pool renames), D2 (carbonate state), E1 (habitat state), and F2 (richer shrimp state) may need to report errors — e.g., when a required source field is missing or has an unexpected type during a rename/transform. Consider changing `MigrationFn` to `fn(&mut Value) -> Result<(), SimError>` now, while there are only two migrations to update (one is a no-op), rather than after more migrations accumulate. The current `migrate_v2_to_v3` already has silent early-returns on missing `state`/`water` that would be better served by returning an error.","rationale":"This is a maintainability risk flagged by the project's own roadmap. The bead spec names 4+ future phases that will add migrations. Changing the signature later means touching every existing migration function. Doing it now costs almost nothing (update the no-op to return `Ok(())`, add `?` to the v2→v3 returns) and gives future contributors a fallible contract from day one."}],"summary":"The save-schema migration scaffolding is well-designed: compile-time assertion enforces migration chain integrity, u32 overflow is handled, future/too-old versions produce clear errors, and the migration contract is thoroughly documented. The v2→v3 migration correctly rescales all 16 dissolved-total fields, and the API handler efficiently uses `from_value`. Test coverage is strong for the migration system itself. Two issues found: (1) a failing test (`hatch_produces_juveniles`) that was modified in this branch but now fails due to chemistry model changes causing toxic ammonia levels, and (2) the infallible migration function signature will need to become fallible for the planned future migrations — cheaper to fix now than later."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"d5ca9522-1376-4e26-aa5e-2b1a74069456"}
